    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "If donor and recurrent-parent DNAs are sufficiently different, recombination between donor and recurrent chromosomes can be greatly reduced, resulting in a much longer segment of donor DNA being retained than expected (e.g., Young and Tanksley 1989a, Paterson et al. 1990). In these cases, the above expressions may give rather serious underestimates. For example, Young and Tanksley (1989a) examined the length of donor DNA introgressed by selecting for a donor marker (resistance to tobacco mosaic virus) in a cross between two species of tomatoes.",
      "source_page": 425,
      "printed_page": 405,
